{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-01",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B044",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration from B013 to B044. The quantum spin Hall effect remains a profound theory–experiment achievement, but the unresolved boundary with previously rewarded topological phases is decisive at this final stage. Optical lattice clocks instead join a specific physical insight—magic-wavelength confinement—with a mature, independently reproduced capability for precision measurement and fundamental tests. Katori and Ye provide a compact and complementary allocation between the originating concept and defining realization. This is not merely improved timekeeping: it made controlled ensembles of atoms into a qualitatively powerful probe of physics.",
    "case_against": "B044 must remain restricted to the invention and realization of the magic-wavelength optical lattice-clock architecture, rather than become an award for optical clocks, frequency metrology, or later performance advances generally. The two-person allocation also requires a final historical check for foundational contributions that could materially alter its fairness.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the lattice-clock achievement has a sharply delimited conceptual and experimental core, while warning against absorbing the broader history of optical-frequency metrology.",
        "response": "I agree. That delimitation resolves my earlier concern that the achievement might be only an incremental metrological advance: magic-wavelength lattice confinement is a distinct physical architecture with demonstrated consequences, provided the citation remains as narrow as Johansson proposes."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson regards aberration-corrected electron optics as a decades-proven transformation of atomic-scale measurement, but identifies a material unresolved allocation among theory, practical correction, validation, and wider implementation.",
        "response": "B010 remains an excellent alternative, especially from the perspective of materials physics and reliable quantitative observation. I now give B044 the edge because its central physical principle and its realization can be more cleanly assigned to the proposed pair; B010 needs stronger documentary resolution of its three-person boundary before it can displace it."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors KPZ for its fundamental universal mechanism and distinguishes that kind of conceptual discovery from an exceptionally capable measurement platform.",
        "response": "That distinction is valuable, but it should not exclude B044. The lattice-clock architecture is itself a precise quantum-control insight, and its reproducible realization has created new physical observables rather than only improved an existing instrument. The remaining concern is attribution and scope, not scientific maturity."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a historical priority review confirms that Katori and Ye alone fairly delimit the originating lattice-clock invention and realization.",
      "Whether the citation can remain strictly confined to magic-wavelength optical lattice clocks and avoid crediting the broader optical-frequency-metrology lineage."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
